WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.444
An equation asserts that two quantities are equal

00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:09.153
Here we seek the input whose output under the rule x plus four is nine

00:00:09.153 --> 00:00:14.100
On the graph, that means locating the intersection with the horizontal level nine.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.545
Subtracting four from both sides preserves equality and reverses the addition

00:00:20.545 --> 00:00:22.883
The unknown is therefore five

00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:30.367
Substituting five into the original equation confirms the result: five plus four really does equal nine.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:36.956
For a multiplication equation, divide both sides by the same nonzero multiplier

00:00:36.956 --> 00:00:40.481
Three x equals twelve becomes x equals four

00:00:40.481 --> 00:00:47.533
The original graph also places the intersection at input four, connecting the algebra with the picture.

00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:52.748
Division by zero cannot be used as an inverse step

00:00:52.748 --> 00:00:57.445
Zero times any real input stays zero, so it never reaches five

00:00:57.445 --> 00:01:04.100
This equation has no solution because its two sides cannot agree, not because the answer is zero.
